    6758	## §H. Cross-x Amortisation — Beyond the Per-Query Obstruction
    6759	
    6760	**Why this section exists:** Threads 1-4 of the commit-mode arc closed
    6761	the per-query polylog frontier. S202 WRAP (Thread 3 / Galway frontier)
    6762	established that K*(x, p) = Θ̃(x) zeros are required for π(x) ± 1 in
    6763	distribution under Montgomery pair-correlation. **But that's a single-x
    6764	result.** Riemann zeros are properties of ζ, shared across all x. The
    6765	open question — explicitly flagged in S202 WRAP as a "legitimate
    6766	falsifier of Thread 3 closure, noted as open but not needed" — is
    6767	whether *batched* π(x_1), ..., π(x_M) queries can have *amortised*
    6768	per-x cost that's polylog. This section is its concrete realisation.
    6769	
    6770	### H1 — Per-pillar cross-x amortisation profile
    6771	
    6772	**Question:** for each of the three pillars (analytic / explicit
    6773	formula, combinatorial / Meissel-Lehmer-HKM, prime-pillar /
    6774	Aggarwal-via-π), what fraction of the per-query work is amortisable
    6775	across M batched queries `π(x_1), ..., π(x_M)`?
    6776	
    6777	For the explicit-formula pillar concretely: per-query cost decomposes
    6778	into `K_zeros_setup + K_per_x_evaluation`. K zeros must be computed
    6779	to fixed precision (`K_zeros_setup`); each x then evaluates
    6780	`Σ_{ρ ≤ K} Li(x^ρ)` (`K_per_x_evaluation`). The setup is amortisable
    6781	across M; the evaluation is not. **Open question:** can
    6782	`K_per_x_evaluation = polylog(x)` while `K_zeros_setup = Θ̃(x_max)`?
    6783	If yes, M-batched amortised cost per x is `Θ̃(x_max)/M + polylog(x)`,
    6784	which is polylog when `M ≥ Θ̃(x_max)/polylog(x_max)`.
    6785	
    6786	**Why frontier:** S202 explicitly noted this as an open falsifier of
    6787	Thread 3. Galway 2004 unconditional and Hiary 2011 single-x bounds
